WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.020
- [Man] This video is
brought to you by Wix,

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.623
my choice for building
LegalEagle's website.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.410
- I'm not a bad guy.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.950
- I know that.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.290
And there's emotional testimony,

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.880
I assume 85% of it is exaggeration.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.040
- And the other 15?

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.670
- Perjury.

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.087
- Accurate.

00:00:17.087 --> 00:00:19.670
(upbeat music)

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.910
Hey LegalEagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.450
Today we are covering The Social Network,

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.440
which is a movie that I'm
told is incredibly realistic.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.870
And in fact I'm told
that some of the dialogue

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:35.620
in this movie comes directly
out of the deposition testimony

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.330
that was taken in the various lawsuits

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.220
that were filed against Facebook
founder Mark Zuckerberg.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.210
So this will be really
interesting from my perspective

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.720
because I've taken a lot of depositions

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.710
and they have a kind of sound to them

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.100
so it'll be interesting to see
if this movie gets it right.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.730
As always, be sure to comment
in the form of an objection,

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:53.790
which I'll either sustain or overrule.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.440
And stick around until
the end of the video

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.730
where I give The Social Network
a grade for legal realism.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:00.750
So without further ado,

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.180
let's dig in to The Social Network.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.030
- So you were called in
front of the ad board.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.230
- That's not what happened.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.680
- You weren't called in
front of the administrator?

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.180
- Alright, so as expected,

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.660
this movie deals a lot with depositions.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.200
What is a deposition?

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.160
A deposition is

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.140
an out-of-court
cross-examination essentially.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.660
Often people think about the
only time you're under oath

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.870
as being on the witness stand in trial.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.350
That's actually not true.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.040
Most of the testimony that
takes place under oath

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.050
takes place in a deposition.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.220
As you can see in this particular scene,

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:32.290
there is a conference room

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.620
which is where most
depositions take place.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.630
And on one side of the
conference room table,

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.840
you have the plaintiff
and all of his attorneys.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.690
In a major case, you're likely
to get two, three, four,

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:43.810
sometimes five attorneys for each side.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.530
It's not uncommon to have the
entire conference room packed

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.850
with attorneys if the
stakes are high enough.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.440
And on the other side of the table,

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.940
you also have Mark Zuckerberg,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.910
in this case the defendant
with his attorneys.

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:58.610
Separating the two sides
is the court reporter.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.240
You can see her in the middle here.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.350
She has her laptop open

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.330
but she is typing into a
stenographer's keyboard

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.230
which is a phonetic keyboard

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.440
that allows her to type very, very fast.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.970
What would have happened
before the scene is

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.990
the stenographer would
have asked Mark Zuckerberg

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.820
to raise his right hand
and swear to tell the truth

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.300
and so essentially put him under oath

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.450
so that the testimony that
he gives is sworn testimony

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.080
and if he lies under oath,

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.670
then effectively he can
be charged with perjury.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.820
A deposition is part of
the discovery process.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.260
What you see on TV with the trial

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.220
is only the tip of the iceberg.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.130
There are hundreds if
not thousands of hours

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:38.170
that go into any particular trial

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.470
of discovery where you're
trying to get the facts

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.890
to support your case so that
you're not blindsided at trial.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.640
And a deposition is one of
the major tools that you have

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.690
in order to get the
testimony from the other side

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:51.580
so you know what they're going to say.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.090
It is possible for someone

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.200
to change their testimony
at trial but if you do,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.410
then you can, quote, "impeach them"

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.170
with the testimony that they
gave during their deposition.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.520
So what we're seeing here
is a spot-on representation

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.450
of what a deposition looks like

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.940
in a multi-million dollar case.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.950
So so far so good, this is very accurate.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:11.840
- She said all that?

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.038
- Mark.

00:03:13.038 --> 00:03:13.871
- [Mark] That I said that stuff to her?

00:03:13.871 --> 00:03:15.640
- I was reading from the
transcript of her deposition.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.260
- Why would you even need to depose her?

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.337
- That's really for us to decide.

00:03:18.337 --> 00:03:20.630
- You think if I know she
can make me look like a jerk,

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.040
I'll be more likely to settle.
- Mark.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:24.580
- Yeah, so it is proper

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.110
to take someone else's
deposition testimony

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.680
and ask another witness
what they think about it.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:30.870
This is not an impeachment.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:35.200
She's not contradicting Mark
Zuckerburg's testimony here.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.500
She is simply providing
the other testimony

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.910
to get the witness's reaction to it.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.120
When you see a cross-examination on TV,

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.850
it's often very, very heated

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.810
but the rules of a deposition are such

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.510
that you can basically ask
whatever question you want.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.360
The only thing you're
really not allowed to ask

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.160
in a deposition are
questions that are protected

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.660
by privilege but that doesn't
seem to be the case here.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.060
- Why don't we stretch
our legs for a minute?

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:58.893
Can we do that?

00:03:58.893 --> 00:04:00.160
It's been almost three hours

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.410
and frankly you did spend
an awful lot of time

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:03.730
embarrassing Mr. Zuckerberg

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.300
with the girl's testimony from the bar.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.200
- I'm not embarrassed, she
just made a lot of that up.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.380
- She was under oath.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:09.590
- And I guess that would be the first time

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:11.370
somebody's lied under oath.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.610
- So this may very well
have been taken right out

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:16.640
of the deposition transcript
because it's often the case

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.690
where an attorney will
ask to take a break,

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:20.670
whether it's for lunch

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.090
or whether just to stretch your legs,

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:24.660
that is exactly the kind of
thing that a defense lawyer,

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.940
a good one, will do so
that you can have time

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.970
to break up the flow of the questions

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:30.580
for the plaintiff's attorney

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.240
and really just to take a break

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.340
because it's very, very mentally taxing

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.280
to be on the other end of a deposition.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.080
And the reason I say this
might be taken right out

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.670
of the deposition transcript is

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:41.800
at no time did any of
the lawyers say okay,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:42.860
we're gonna go off the record,

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.440
which is a signal to the stenographer

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.240
to stop taking down everything.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.980
And unless you tell the
stenographer to stop typing,

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:52.350
she will continue to take
down everything that you say,

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.650
including this sort of mundane colloquy

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:57.160
between the defense attorney
and the plaintiff's attorney

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.890
over whether they're
gonna take a break or not

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.800
and then Mark Zuckerburg's
alleged kind of snippy dialogue

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.180
where he's talking back to
the plaintiff's attorney.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.500
All of that could very
well be on the record

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.610
because no one's said
to go off of the record,

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.710
which is what you would normally say.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:15.050
So I think this could be
very real testimony here.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:19.940
- Cameron Winklevoss, W-I-N-K-L-E-V-O-S-S.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.120
Cameron's spelled the usual way.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.790
- Tyler Winklevoss.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:23.930
Tyler spelled the usual way

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:26.250
and my last name is the
same as my brother's.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.110
- Okay, here we have another deposition,

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:30.320
this time by the Winklevoss twins.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.340
It would be very, very
unusual to have a deposition

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.540
where you questioned two people at once.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.350
That's probably more for dramatic effect.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:38.590
I doubt that they took both

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:40.820
of their depositions simultaneously.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:44.070
But if you go back to the
beginning of that scene,

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.560
you get a really good view

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.330
of what the stenographer is doing.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:50.700
She has a special keyboard,
it's hooked up to a laptop,

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.190
and it writes everything
down in special software.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:54.660
If you are an attorney,

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.470
you can actually get a live
readout of what's going on

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.210
and often that becomes
very, very important

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.870
to make sure that the
exact words that you want,

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:03.700
especially when you're
dealing with a contract case

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.150
where the words matter,

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:07.820
but to make sure that
the exact words come out

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:09.730
and are actually in the official record

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:12.490
because if the stenographer
didn't hear it,

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:14.650
that really doesn't exist anymore.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:17.060
So the stenographer is
very, very important

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:19.080
in terms of a deposition.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.370
And as you can kind of see

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.600
on the stenographer's laptop screen,

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.390
almost everything that you
get out of a deposition

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.490
is written in the font Courier.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:27.410
I have no idea why.

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.010
It's kind of hard to read

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:30.990
and it looks really, really archaic

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.830
but like screenwriters and screenplays,

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:34.940
almost everything you get
out of a deposition is

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.790
in this Courier font.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:38.220
Who knew?

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.200
- That's what you said?

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.460
- It was three or four years ago,

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.710
I don't know what I said.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:44.879
- When did you come to Eduardo?

00:06:44.879 --> 00:06:45.712
- I don't understand that question.

00:06:45.712 --> 00:06:48.840
- Do you remember answering
in the affirmative?

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:49.910
- The affirmative?

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:52.450
- When did you come to Eduardo
with the idea for Facebook?

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:54.370
- It was called The Facebook then.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.663
- This doesn't need to be that difficult.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:58.803
- This is actually kind of a clever thing

00:06:58.803 --> 00:07:00.350
that they're doing in this movie.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.300
They're using Mark Zuckerberg's testimony

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.780
in two different lawsuits,

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:07.470
two depositions spaced
probably months or years apart

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.910
and comparing his
testimony between the two.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:12.610
And in fact in the later
deposition that takes place

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:15.110
in the darker room, what he's doing is

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:17.480
he's reading back Zuckerberg's testimony

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:19.000
from the prior lawsuit.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.140
Often people's prior
testimony will hurt them

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:23.730
in other lawsuits because
the testimony that they gave

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.120
is good for a particular lawsuit

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.870
but it might be bad
for a different lawsuit

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:28.840
that hinges on other facts and law.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:30.620
What this shows is also a funny phenomenon

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.560
that happens often in depositions

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.060
where a witness has been over-coached,

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.500
where the witness has
been told you don't want

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.720
to give in to questions even
if they're easy answers.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.280
And what Zuckerberg has
done here is he has refused

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:46.880
to answer basically that he answered yes

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.940
to a prior question that
really isn't in dispute.

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:51.560
If you want to see a
great example of this,

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:54.070
check out a video called
What Is a Photo Copier

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:55.310
put out by the New York Times

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:58.620
which is a dramatization of a
deposition just like this one

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:01.720
where someone refuses
to answer the question

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:05.280
of what is a copy machine
or what is a photo copier.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.683
- When you say photocopying
machine, what do you mean?

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:13.690
- They were essentially coached too well

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:14.860
and they refused to answer

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.930
even the most simple mundane questions

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.150
and the lawyer just goes apoplectic.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.320
- Do you have such a machine?

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:22.153
- Yes sir.

00:08:22.153 --> 00:08:25.270
- What do you call that machine?

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:28.500
- Sometimes it's really hard
to get even very simple answers

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.150
from a highly coached witness.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:32.550
- There was a big project

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.830
and he was going to have
to write tens of thousands

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.230
of lines of code so I wondered
why he was coming to me

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:38.520
and not his roommates.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.700
Dustin Moskovitz and Chris
Hughes, they were programmers.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.780
- This scene doesn't
make a whole lot of sense

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:46.460
because this appears to
be the exact same scene

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:48.180
of Zuckerberg's deposition.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.000
The plaintiff's lawyer is asking questions

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.330
of Zuckerberg but it
doesn't make any sense

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:54.370
for Eduardo Saverin to be testifying

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:57.860
at exactly the same
deposition as Mark Zuckerberg.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:59.580
What I think the filmmakers have done here

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.890
is combined two different depositions.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:04.100
They're combining the
deposition of Zuckerberg

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:07.320
with the deposition of
Saverin into the same thing.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:10.570
But you only get so many
hours to depose the witness

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:12.050
that you want to depose

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:14.190
so you would never take up that time

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:17.300
by interviewing another
witness on the same day

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.100
at the same time if this
was a very crucial witness

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.310
as the deposition of
Mark Zuckerberg would be.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:24.420
So I think they just
combined the two together

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:26.220
and it's not realistic

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:29.700
that Saverin is testifying
essentially in the middle

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.363
of Zuckerberg's testimony here.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.120
- This is the first time
he mentioned any problem.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:35.953
- Yes, it was.

00:09:35.953 --> 00:09:38.750
- You sent 36 emails to Mr. Zuckerberg

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:40.860
and received 16 emails in return

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.500
and this was the first time
he indicated he was not happy.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:44.540
- That's correct.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:47.420
You had 42 days to study your
system and get out ahead.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:48.900
- Do you see any of your code on Facebook?

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:49.820
- Sy, could you--

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.270
- Did I use any of your code?

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:52.840
- You stole our whole (beeps) idea.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:53.673
- Fellas.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.920
- So it's possible that
Zuckerberg may have interjected

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.790
into this deposition of
this other gentleman here.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.800
It's really, really bad practice

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.110
because you don't want
your witness to chime in

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:08.500
on someone else's
witness's testimony here.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.060
But it appears that the lawyers are trying

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:12.820
to get a handle on their
two prospective clients

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:15.880
to try to prevent some
interactions so it's very possible

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:18.410
that this is taken from real deposition

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:20.470
where Zuckerberg just became angry

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:22.960
at what the deponent was saying

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:27.360
and then was forced to be
quiet by his own lawyer.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.380
I should note that it is totally proper

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:32.430
for Zuckerberg to be present
during the deposition

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:33.990
of these other people.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:36.750
In fact, there's many reasons
why you would want to do that.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:37.930
If Zuckerberg is the one

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:39.780
who has all the crucial information,

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:42.500
he may need to provide that information

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:44.930
to his attorney to know
what questions to ask

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:47.550
or to know where people are lying

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:50.560
because you often only get
one shot at these depositions

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.240
and it can't wait for the
testimony to be given back

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:54.910
to your client, to get comments back,

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:56.410
and then get to ask questions later.

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:58.010
That's not really how it works.

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:00.190
So it often makes sense
for the party to be there

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:02.710
in person to give you real-time notes.

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:05.530
And at least with the Federal
Rules of Civil Procedure,

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:07.820
a named party can absolutely be present

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:11.210
during the testimony of
another witness like this.

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:14.570
- During the time when
you say you had this idea,

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.873
did you know Tyler and Cameron
came from a family of means?

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:20.140
- A family of means?

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.140
- Did you know their father was wealthy?

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:24.590
- [Mark] I'm not sure why
you're asking me that.

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:26.620
- It's not important you
be sure why I'm asking.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.080
- It's not important to you.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:28.913
- Sy.

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:32.500
- Yeah, so this might
be taken directly out

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:34.130
of deposition testimony here.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:35.910
The witness does not get to pick

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:38.710
and choose what questions
he or she answers

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:42.270
and whether or not Zuckerberg
was aware of the wealth

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:44.750
of the Winklevoss twins might be relevant

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:46.160
to a further line of questioning,

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:47.670
so you don't get to fight it

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:50.060
just because you think it's irrelevant.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.540
You have to answer the
questions that are given to you.

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:53.750
- I went to my friend for the money

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:55.790
because that's who I
wanted to be partners with.

00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:57.010
Eduardo was the president

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:58.640
of the Harvard Investors Association

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.317
and he was also my best friend.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:03.640
- So I should point out
that in this deposition,

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:06.880
it's very, very common in a
multi-million dollar deposition

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.070
to have it being video recorded.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.390
Part of the value of
asking these questions

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:13.720
that a witness is likely
to say are irrelevant

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:16.310
or just basically foundational is

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:18.580
that you want the witness on camera

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:20.240
fighting you tooth and nail

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:22.080
because often in a deposition,

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.030
the witnesses are not
on their best behavior

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:25.110
because they don't realize

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:27.460
that their testimony might
come back to haunt them.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:30.110
So often they're on their
best behavior in trial

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:31.890
in front of a jury and a judge,

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:34.920
whereas in front of a camera
they forget it's there

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:38.930
and they might be kind of
jerks to the other side

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:40.320
and you might be able to use that

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:42.010
to destroy their credibility later on,

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:44.120
especially where someone refuses

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:45.760
to answer a very simple question

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:49.190
like did you know someone came from wealth

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:51.283
in a situation like this.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.840
- Eduardo, what happened
after the initial launch?

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:55.887
- I'm sorry, Sy,

00:12:55.887 --> 00:12:57.490
would you mind addressing
him as Mr. Saverin?

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:58.640
- Gretchen, they're best friends.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:12:59.473
- Not anymore.

00:12:59.473 --> 00:13:01.250
- We already went through
this on the, never mind.

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:02.083
Mr. Saverin.

00:13:03.295 --> 00:13:04.150
- That's a really funny interaction

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:05.620
between the two attorneys.

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:07.580
There's no rule that requires one attorney

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:11.060
to refer to a witness as
a specific thing or not,

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:13.340
but often you might just interject that

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:17.300
to break up their flow and
to fluster them a little bit

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.683
and get them speaking in your terms

00:13:19.683 --> 00:13:21.420
so that the record reflects things

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.830
the way you want them reflected.

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:26.030
- And Mark was the biggest
thing on the campus

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:29.260
that included 19 Nobel Laureates,
15 Pulitzer Prize winners,

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:33.045
two future Olympians, and a movie star.

00:13:33.045 --> 00:13:34.360
- Who was the movie star?

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.200
- Does it matter?

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.340
- That does not sound
like deposition testimony,

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:40.890
that sounds like something
written by Aaron Sorkin.

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:45.450
- You don't want any lunch?

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:47.000
- No.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.280
- You're welcome to some salad.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:50.990
- This is a really accurate scene so far.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:53.400
All the partners have gone to lunch,

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:55.090
they're out of the office,

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:58.390
leaving one junior associate
to basically play babysitter

00:13:58.390 --> 00:13:59.580
with the client to make sure

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:01.850
that they don't do anything stupid.

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:02.933
- This must be hard.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:06.198
- Who are you?

00:14:06.198 --> 00:14:07.250
- I'm Marylin Delpy.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:08.659
I introduced myself when we first--

00:14:08.659 --> 00:14:10.182
- I mean, what do you do?

00:14:10.182 --> 00:14:12.260
- I'm a second-year associate at the firm.

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:14.810
My boss wanted me to sit
in on the deposition days.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:17.143
- Very accurate.

00:14:17.143 --> 00:14:19.620
The way that junior
associates get experience is

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:22.070
they get to sit in on
depositions like this

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:23.610
so they can see how it's done,

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:25.010
so they can learn the tactics.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.820
And if the client can afford it,

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:30.600
it's a great way for junior
associates to gain experience.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.350
- Well, as CFO, I had set up some meetings

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.160
with potential advertisers.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:36.170
- [Woman] Who paid for the trips?

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:37.980
- Paid for out of the $1,000 account

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:39.510
that I set up a few months earlier.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:42.090
- At this point, your
$1,000 was the only money

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:43.330
that had been put into the company?

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:44.163
- Yes.

00:14:44.163 --> 00:14:45.563
- How did you feel the meetings went?

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:48.410
- They went terribly.

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:49.890
- Again, this doesn't make a lot of sense

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:52.810
because Eduardo Saverin's
lawyer is the one

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:55.220
that's questioning Eduardo Saverin.

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:56.840
Usually that doesn't happen.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:59.820
There's not much to be
gained for creating testimony

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:01.070
from your own client.

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:04.300
The point of a deposition
is to get adverse testimony

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:05.980
from your opponent.

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:07.430
So the plaintiff's lawyer is going

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:08.740
to question Mark Zuckerberg

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:10.200
and Mark Zuckerberg's attorney is going

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:12.150
to question Eduardo Saverin.

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:14.480
It's very rare to get deposition testimony

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:16.240
out of your own client

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.510
because you never know
what kind of stupid things

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:19.730
your client is going to say

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:22.570
when you're not the one
controlling that testimony.

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:24.220
Instead what you would want to do

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:27.300
if you want to get testimony
out to support a motion

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.370
like a summary judgment motion is

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:31.120
to file what's called an affidavit

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.220
which is a written declaration

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:35.820
of what your client swears to.

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:38.060
You can attach exhibits to it,

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.730
you can say whatever you
want as long as it's truthful

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:42.540
and then the client has to sign off on it.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:44.480
So there's really no reason why you want

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.900
to get verbal testimony
from your own client

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:49.660
when you can always get
written testimony later on.

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:51.210
- Going to enter this into the record,

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.010
incorporation papers for Facebook,

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:55.240
an LLC registered in Florida.

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:56.073
Why Florida?

00:15:56.073 --> 00:15:57.390
- It's where my family lives.

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:58.810
- And ask the respondent to stipulate

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:00.180
that the Articles of Incorporation

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:01.580
state the ownership as follows.

00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:05.410
65% for Mark Zuckerberg,
30% for Eduardo Saverin,

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:07.230
and 5% for Dustin Moskovitz.

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:08.767
- [Man] Yeah, we stipulate.

00:16:08.767 --> 00:16:10.660
- [Woman] And that was April 13th, 2004.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:11.920
- You can mark it.

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:14.560
- So this is really, really accurate.

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:15.880
When you're doing a deposition,

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:19.390
you often deal with physical
exhibits all the time.

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:22.430
Emails, articles of
incorporation like this,

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:25.160
any physical paperwork that
needs to be talked about.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:28.270
First you have to mark it as
an exhibit to the deposition.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.290
You of course need to
describe what you're going

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:32.610
to be talking about and
then put a number on it

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:34.240
so that you can always refer back

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:38.820
to, say, exhibit 35 is the
Articles of Incorporation.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:42.300
So the dialogue is going on
right here is a 100% accurate.

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:44.350
Anytime you deal with a physical exhibit

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:46.170
in a deposition you've gotta mark it,

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:47.600
you got to add it to the record,

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:50.210
and then it's not entered
into evidence per se,

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:52.640
it's just an exhibit to that deposition

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:55.880
and often you'll refer back
to that in other depositions

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:58.023
or throughout that particular deposition.

00:16:58.900 --> 00:16:59.800
- [Woman] Eduardo.

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:02.600
- [Eduardo] Can you please
repeat the question?

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:03.960
- No, it was an outrageously
leading question

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:04.793
the first time around

00:17:04.793 --> 00:17:05.650
and now you want us to hear it twice?

00:17:05.650 --> 00:17:08.280
- Yes, would you read it back please?

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:09.113
- Go ahead.

00:17:09.113 --> 00:17:10.920
- Counsel, and when you
signed these documents,

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:11.753
were you aware

00:17:11.753 --> 00:17:13.570
that you were signing your
own death certificate?

00:17:13.570 --> 00:17:15.080
- No.

00:17:15.080 --> 00:17:16.260
- That's a great tactic.

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.730
So the stenographer, as
I already talked about,

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.810
is constantly taking down everything

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:23.990
and often the opposing attorney
won't like the questions

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:25.100
that you are asking.

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:28.250
And one way to get around that
just as a rhetorical device

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:31.770
is to ask the stenographer
to read back the question

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:32.603
that you asked.

00:17:32.603 --> 00:17:34.670
And often as what happens here,

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:36.590
the witness actually
just answers the question

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:38.430
without you having to re-answer it

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:40.670
and without the other
attorney objecting to it.

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:42.050
Here you could kind of hear

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:43.930
that the opposing attorney was objecting

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:46.360
to the question as misleading.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:48.700
It's not usually a proper objection.

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:51.940
The rules of evidence don't
really apply in the deposition,

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.090
you're allowed to ask
whatever questions you want.

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:55.100
And often when I'm dealing

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:57.080
with obstreperous opposing counsel,

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:58.220
I'll do the exact same thing.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:17:59.790
I'll ignore the opposing counsel

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:01.940
and just simply ask the court reporter

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.550
to read back the question
that I originally asked

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:07.253
and I will often get an
answer to my question.

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:10.040
- I'm not a bad guy.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:11.600
- I know that.

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:12.900
And there's emotional testimony,

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:15.500
I assume 85% of it is exaggeration.

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:16.660
- And the other 15?

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:18.280
- Perjury.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:19.690
- Accurate.

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:20.773
- What happens now?

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:24.960
- Sy and the others are having a steak

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:28.450
on University Avenue and they'll
come back up to the office

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:30.330
and start working on
a settlement agreement

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:31.277
to present to you.

00:18:32.174 --> 00:18:33.007
- We're gonna settle?

00:18:33.007 --> 00:18:34.380
- Oh yeah.

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:36.270
And you're gonna have
to pay a little extra.

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:37.103
- Why?

00:18:37.103 --> 00:18:39.570
- So that these guys sign
a nondisclosure agreement.

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:41.620
They say one unflattering
word about you in public,

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:42.650
you own their wife and kids.

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:44.570
- I invented Facebook.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.740
- Yeah, so there's some truth to that.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:49.610
I think that generally what
happens after a deposition

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:52.210
is not going straight to settlement.

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:53.840
Really what's gonna happen is a motion

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:54.740
for summary judgment,

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:57.310
which is basically like
a trial but on paper.

00:18:57.310 --> 00:18:59.030
You take all of the depositions,

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:00.760
you take all of the exhibits,

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:02.040
you put them down on paper,

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:03.500
you make your most convincing argument,

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:06.170
and you say that even taking the evidence

00:19:06.170 --> 00:19:08.010
in the light most favorable
to the other party

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:11.780
that you're against, it
still supports your side

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:13.300
as a matter of law.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:14.500
In the grand scheme of things,

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:17.060
the $500,000 that it takes

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:20.150
to put together a summary
judgment motion is nothing

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:21.590
compared to the billions of dollars

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:24.420
that are at stake and potentially at stake

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:27.930
in this settlement agreement
or the lawsuit in general.

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:30.380
So most likely the next step
would be summary judgment

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:32.370
and only then would you
go after settlement.

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:33.710
And if settlement failed,

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:34.780
then you would go after trial.

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:38.990
That's why 98% of cases settle
first before going to trial

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:40.510
because the stakes are too high

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:43.123
and you never know which
way a jury is going to go.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:45.870
- I have been licensed to practice law

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:48.490
for all of 20 months and I
could get a jury to believe

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:51.270
that you planted the story
about Eduardo and the chicken.

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:52.910
Watch what else.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:55.720
Why weren't you at Shawn's
sorority party that night?

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:57.230
- You think I'm the one
that called the police.

00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:58.063
- Doesn't matter.

00:19:58.063 --> 00:20:00.090
I asked a question, now
everybody's thinking about it.

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:02.390
You've lost your jury
in the first 10 minutes.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:06.060
- Some truth to that.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:09.910
- I was drunk and angry and stupid.

00:20:09.910 --> 00:20:10.810
- And blogging.

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:11.683
- And blogging.

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:14.880
- Pay them.

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:18.370
In the scheme of things,
it's a speeding ticket.

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:20.750
- Yeah, for better or
worse, that's often true.

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:22.750
For a huge corporation,

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:26.130
making these kind of settlements
is an easier thing to do

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:28.530
than to roll the dice and go to trial

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:30.300
where you might lose hundreds of millions

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:32.320
or billions of dollars.

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:34.903
(upbeat music)

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:38.060
Alright, that was The Social Network.

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:40.614
Now let's give it a
grade for legal realism.

00:20:40.614 --> 00:20:43.410
(gavel banging)

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:46.210
So much of this movie is
told through depositions

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:48.200
and it appears that a lot of the dialogue

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:50.910
was taken directly out of
the deposition testimony.

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:52.950
You can tell when there's back and forth

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:55.890
between these lawyers or
they're asking for a lunch break

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:57.610
or they've been going for too long.

00:20:57.610 --> 00:20:59.410
That's exactly what a lawyer would say

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:00.960
and the dialogue is spot-on,

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:04.420
right down to marking an
exhibit to go into the record.

00:21:04.420 --> 00:21:06.080
I don't know that I've
ever seen a depiction

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:09.180
of a deposition more accurate
than what we saw here.

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.050
On the other hand,

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:12.650
I think that there is some
cinematic license taken

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:15.490
where they are conflating
different depositions together,

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:18.630
they're having the wrong
attorney ask the questions here.

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:21.450
The witness at one point
was turned completely around

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:22.630
and wasn't even looking at the camera.

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:24.030
That would never be allowed.

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.510
It's all for dramatic effect.

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:26.370
But notwithstanding,

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:29.670
this was one of if not the
most accurate legal drama

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:31.450
I have ever seen in my entire life.

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:34.590
I think largely because they
used the deposition testimony

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:37.300
as the reference material
for the movie itself.

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:39.160
So with all of that under consideration,

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:41.510
I'm giving The Social Network an A-minus.

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:43.980
It's clear you did well at Harvard.

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:44.990
